<div class="data-source">
<p class="citation">Sevostianov, Igor and Kachanov, Mark (2009). Connections between elastic and conductive properties of heterogeneous materials. Advances in Applied Mechanics 42, 69-252. <a target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" href="https://doi.org/10.1016/S0065-2156(08)00002-1">doi:10.1016/S0065-2156(08)00002-1</a></p>
<p><span class="label">Read in</span> Section 7.1, pp. 183-187 (Tables 7.1 and 7.2, Eq. (7.4) and the text below it); section 7.5.2, pp. 204-206 (Eqs. (7.15)-(7.16), Table 7.13) <span class="badge ok">checked against the source</span></p>
<p><span class="label">Transcribed from</span> the published chapter (PDF), tables read on the page images and compared value by value with the text layer</p>
<p class="note">Aluminum foam AlMg1Si0.6: the conductivity (Table 7.1) and the Young modulus (Table 7.2) were measured on different specimens; the porosity is the volume fraction of pores, the last row of each table (porosity 0) is the bulk alloy. The coefficients psi are quoted in the text below Eq. (7.4): 1.33 for spherical pores and 1.34 for randomly oriented pores of aspect ratio 0.7 at nu0 = 0.33. YSZ coatings (Table 7.13): E1 is the Young modulus along the substrate, measured by laser ultrasound, k3 the thermal conductivity across it, from laser-flash diffusivity; the uncertainties are those printed with each value; &#39;E1_predicted&#39; is the column computed by the authors from Eq. (7.16) with alpha11/alpha33 = 1.4. The same data are in Kachanov and Sevostianov (2018), sections 7.1 and 7.5.</p>
</div>
